Tell a new web process whether ad blocking is on.

lib/adblock.lua sends a newly created web process its rules and each list's
enabled flag, but never the module's own `enabled' state.  The web module
declares that state as `local enabled = true' and only learns otherwise from
an "enable" signal, emitted once, to the processes that exist at the time the
user runs `:adblock-disable'.

So `:abd' turns blocking off in the processes that are running and in none of
the ones created afterwards.  Open a page that makes luakit spawn or swap a
web process and that page is filtered again, with nothing in the UI to say
so -- which is exactly what made the command look like it does nothing when
it was used to test whether the blocker was breaking a site.

Sending the state alongside the rules costs one message and makes the command
mean what it says.

Upstream: applies to luakit 2.4.0 as shipped; worth reporting.

--- a/lib/adblock.lua	2025-02-14 13:18:26.000000000 +0100
+++ b/lib/adblock.lua	2026-09-14 22:40:28.434494414 +0200
@@ -490,6 +490,11 @@
 luakit.add_signal("web-extension-created", function (view)
     new_web_extension_created = true
     adblock_wm:emit_signal(view, "update_rules", _M.rules)
+    -- ...and whether blocking is on at all.  Without this the new process
+    -- keeps the web module's own default (true) and goes on filtering after
+    -- `:adblock-disable', which is what made that command look like it does
+    -- nothing when it was used to test whether the blocker broke a site.
+    adblock_wm:emit_signal(view, "enable", _M.enabled)
     for name, list in pairs(_M.rules) do
         local enabled = util.table.hasitem(list.opts, "Enabled")
         adblock_wm:emit_signal(view, "list_set_enabled", name, enabled)
